# BUILD SLOTHFLOW: AN OPEN-WEIGHT AI PRODUCTIVITY ASSISTANT

Act as a senior full-stack engineer, AI application architect, UI/UX designer, and developer advocate.

Build a complete, functional, responsive web application named **SlothFlow — The Lazy-Day AI Assistant**.

Do not merely provide a plan, wireframe, or code snippets. Create the actual working application in the current Google AI Studio Build workspace, generate all necessary files, implement the features, run the available checks, and fix errors.

The application must use **Gemma 4 as its runtime AI model**. Do not silently substitute Gemini or another proprietary model when a Gemma request fails.

The application must also include a working DEV.to article creation workflow so that its owner can generate a technical blog post about the project, edit it, save it as a draft, or explicitly publish it to DEV.to.

# 1. PROJECT VISION

Application name: SlothFlow

Tagline: "Do less busywork. Keep your brain for better things."

Project theme: Slow and Steady — building with open-weight models or open-source AI that rewards laziness.

Primary concept:

SlothFlow is a lightweight productivity assistant that removes unnecessary mental work.

Users often have scattered thoughts, long notes, messy to-do lists, difficult messages to reply to, and too many small tasks competing for their attention.

SlothFlow allows them to provide that unorganized input and uses Gemma 4 to produce useful, concise, actionable results.

The application should favor small steps, less cognitive effort, realistic schedules, and practical automation instead of overwhelming users with complicated productivity systems.

The project must demonstrate why open AI innovation matters, especially model choice, local inference, privacy, offline capability, portability, and the ability to customize the system.

Do not falsely claim that all deployment modes are offline or private. Clearly distinguish between local inference and hosted inference.

